  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ is the left null space of $B.$ This consists of vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${B}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$\n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${B}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${B}^{T}$, that is, each column of $B$, by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the column space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Col}(B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $B$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14100016/assets/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)",
